  "provider_query": "Stack Overflow question page about creating a Python virtual environment",
  "answer": "Relevant Stack Overflow threads on creating a Python virtual environment:\n\n1. **[How to create virtual env with Python 3?](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/43069780/how-to-create-virtual-env-with-python-3)** — Top answers use `python3 -m venv ./path-to-new-venv` (stdlib `venv`) and also `virtualenv -p python3 venv_name`. Covers activation (`source .venv/bin/activate`) and deactivation.\n\n2. **[What is a virtualenv, and why should I use one?](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/41972261/what-is-a-virtualenv-and-why-should-i-use-one)** — Explains isolation from the system Python. Creation: `python3 -m venv ENV_DIR` (3.3+); older options include `virtualenv`, `pyvenv`. Activation differs on Unix vs Windows (`venv\\Scripts\\activate`).\n\n3. **[How to create a venv with a different Python version](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/70422866/how-to-create-a-venv-with-a-different-python-version)** — Use the interpreter you want: `python3.9 -m venv ~/.venvs/my-venv-name`, or Windows `py -3.7 -m venv test_env`. Also `virtualenv -p <path-to-python>`. Notes Debian/Ubuntu may need `python3.x-venv`.\n\n4. **[python3 -m venv: how to specify Python point release/version?](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/54700307/python3-m-venv-how-to-specify-python-point-release-version)** — `venv` uses whichever Python you invoke (`python3.6 -m venv whatever`). `virtualenv --python=python3.6 .venv` is the third-party alternative. Mentions `pyenv` workflows.\n\n5. **[How to create Python Virtual environment within a python script](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/57921255/how-to-create-python-virtual-environment-within-a-python-script)** — Programmatic creation with `venv.create(dir, with_pip=True)` or `virtualenv.create_environment(venv_dir)`, then `pip` via subprocess.\n\n6. **[Create Virtual Environment using Python Documentation](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/56339125/create-virtual-environment-using-python-documentation)** — Docs-style `python3 -m venv env`. Troubleshooting “ensurepip is not available” on Debian/Ubuntu with `apt install python3.x-venv`.\n\n7. **[How to use virtualenv with Python?](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/35017160/how-to-use-virtualenv-with-python)** — Install order Python → pip → `virtualenv`, then `virtualenv env` (or `virtualenv -p /path/to/python venv`), activate, and `pip install`.\n\n8. **[How to create a virtual environment in Python using an environment.yaml file?](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/68104229/how-to-create-a-virtual-environment-in-python-using-an-environment-yaml-file)** — YAML files are usually **conda**, not stdlib `venv`: `conda env create -f environment.yml`. For plain venv, answers point to `requirements.txt` plus `pip install -r`.",
